    <node><name>2</name><description>A stretch</description><answertest>AlgEquiv</answertest><sans>ans1</sans><tans>dSt</tans><testoptions></testoptions><quiet>1</quiet><truescoremode>=</truescoremode><truescore>0.0000000</truescore><truepenalty></truepenalty><truenextnode>-1</truenextnode><trueanswernote>prt1-3-T</trueanswernote><truefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>[[if test="is(k=1)"]]That is the identity matrix: it leaves every point where it is.[[else]]That matrix multiplies one coordinate by \({@k@}\), as a stretch does, so it moves points at right angles to the axis.[[/if]] A shear slides every point parallel to the invariant axis.</p>]]></text></truefeedback><falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode><falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore><falsepenalty></falsepenalty><falsenextnode>-1</falsenextnode><falseanswernote>prt1-3-F</falseanswernote><falsefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Your matrix sends (1, 0) to \(({@ans1[1,1]@},\,{@ans1[2,1]@})\) and (0, 1) to \(({@ans1[1,2]@},\,{@ans1[2,2]@})\). Find the images of (1, 0) and (0, 1) under the shear: they are the columns of the matrix. The one on the [[if test="is(ax=\"x\")"]]x[[else]]y[[/if]]-axis does not move; the other slides parallel to the [[if test="is(ax=\"x\")"]]x[[else]]y[[/if]]-axis.</p>]]></text></falsefeedback></node>
